# Baseline for tests\autotest\run_resolver_version_guard.ps1
#
# <DRAGLINT_RESOLVER_VERSION>|<sha256 of the resolve surface>
#
# The resolve surface is the set of routines listed in tests\resolver-surface.txt
# -- the code that DERIVES call_edges, type_ancestors, type_helpers and
# unit_uses targets from parses the index already holds. Change any of it and
# this guard fails until DRAGLINT_RESOLVER_VERSION is bumped, because every
# existing index would otherwise keep edges from the OLD resolver.
#
# THE REMEDY IS CHEAP, and that is the whole point of a second stamp. A bump of
# DRAGLINT_EXTRACTOR_VERSION costs a ~5 hour re-parse of every database; a bump
# of THIS one costs a re-resolve measured in minutes. They were conflated until
# 2026-08-31, which over-billed 19 commits under src\index and under-billed 42
# under src\storage -- the latter going silently stale, one every 2.2 days.
#
# EXACTLY ONE ACTIVE LINE. The sibling extractor baseline was APPENDED to rather
# than replaced on 2026-08-26 and its guard, which reads only the first active
# line, became structurally incapable of failing for five days. This guard
# asserts its own baseline's shape for that reason. Move superseded lines into
# `#` history below.
#
# To update: bump the constant in src\core\DRagLint.Core.Model.pas, re-run the
# guard, paste the line it prints. If a change genuinely cannot affect what is
# derived, update the hash WITHOUT bumping and say why here -- the same
# discipline the extractor baseline uses, and the same warning: the failure mode
# of getting it wrong is SILENT.
#
# 2026-08-31: created. Seeded at 1.0.0-alpha rather than mirroring the extractor
#   version, because this stamp has its own history from here and pretending it
#   shares the extractor's past would be false. Existing indexes carry no
#   resolver_fingerprint at all and are grandfathered by the same rule the
#   indexer fingerprint uses -- an absent stamp adopts the current one silently
#   rather than forcing a mass re-resolve on first run.

# 2026-08-31: 1.0.0-alpha -> 1.1.0-alpha. UpsertCallEdge now writes
#   refs.symbol_id for a CERTAIN call edge, and ClearCallEdges nulls the column
#   it wrote. That column has been NULL on every row of every index while
#   INDEX-SCHEMA.md documented a join on it, so every consumer of `refs` was
#   name-joining whether it meant to or not. This is a genuine change to what
#   the resolve pass DERIVES, so every index owes one re-resolve -- minutes,
#   not the ~5 hours a re-parse would cost, which is the entire reason this
#   stamp is separate from DRAGLINT_EXTRACTOR_VERSION.
#
#   Superseded: 1.0.0-alpha|ae753fb79518fac5b932d8e9f6d78fc0fce625248ae44c846ac4ae6a57b805be

# 2026-09-06 (C1b): HASH UPDATED, VERSION DELIBERATELY NOT BUMPED -- this is the
#   "genuinely cannot affect what is derived" case the header describes, and it
#   is recorded here in full because the header also says the failure mode of
#   getting it wrong is SILENT.
#
#   What changed inside the surface: ONE line was added to ResolveAncestry,
#   `FFlowOracles.Clear;`, beside the existing `FAnchorCache.Clear;`. Nothing
#   else in the manifest's routines was touched. (The other edits to
#   Storage.SQLite.pas in the same commit -- a field, its create/free, and a
#   one-line getter -- are all OUTSIDE every listed routine.)
#
#   Why it cannot change what is derived, checked rather than asserted:
#   FFlowOracles is a TFlowOracleCache, the C1b store-lifetime memo for the five
#   LINT-time flow oracles. Its contents are read in exactly one unit,
#   DRagLint.Diagnostics.FlowChecks.pas, and only from TFlowChecker.Check's
#   dynamic extent. Inside Storage.SQLite.pas the object is only declared,
#   created, freed, returned by FlowOracles, and cleared here. No resolve
#   routine reads it, so ResolveAncestry writes exactly the type_ancestors rows
#   it wrote before. Verified by enumerating every reference to FlowOracles /
#   FFlowOracles in src\ (18 sites; 10 in FlowChecks, 8 structural in storage).
#
#   Bumping instead would have forced a re-resolve of every index for a cleared
#   dictionary -- precisely the OVER-BILLING that splitting this stamp from
#   DRAGLINT_EXTRACTOR_VERSION exists to end, and the same reason
#   ResolveLog/ResolveSecs are EXCLUDEd from the surface.
#
#   Superseded: 1.1.0-alpha|790d7011021acd549cc6420b02884df7470eef4c6a9ece2163405a6fdac3dfa9

# 2026-09-07: HASH UPDATED, VERSION DELIBERATELY NOT BUMPED -- the "genuinely
#   cannot affect what is derived" case, same discipline as the 2026-09-06 (C1b)
#   entry above.
#
#   WHAT CHANGED: the 91-file autodoc sweep (b42a7e7) regenerated managed
#   DocInsight blocks across src\. Two files of this surface carry them --
#   DRagLint.Index.CallResolver.pas (hashed whole, `::*`) and
#   DRagLint.Storage.SQLite.pas (hashed per listed routine).
#
#   PROVEN comment-only, not asserted. `git diff -U0 fef1067 b42a7e7` over both
#   files, classifying every added/removed line:
#
#       doc-comment (///) lines changed : 633
#       NON-doc lines changed           :   0
#
#   Zero. No routine body changed, so every resolve pass derives exactly the
#   call_edges, type_ancestors, type_helpers and unit_uses rows it derived
#   before, and no index owes a re-resolve.
#
#   Note the asymmetry with the extractor baseline's twin entry: the count here
#   is far larger (633 vs 253) only because Storage.SQLite.pas is this repo's
#   most heavily documented unit. Volume is not evidence either way -- the
#   load-bearing number is the ZERO on the second line.
#
#   Same warning as every entry above: the failure mode of getting this wrong is
#   SILENT.
#
#   Superseded: 1.1.0-alpha|3be00d360f91f38761edeb97760b40607a28bce7f24044608cfa9d361399c21e

# 2026-09-17: 1.2.0-alpha -> 1.4.0-alpha (extractor batch, Task 12). Ancestor
#   edges now resolve by ARITY before scope rule 0: FilterCandidatesByArity
#   (new; added to the manifest) narrows the candidate set in ResolveAncestry's
#   edge loop and in ResolveTypeNameToClass.PickCandidate, and
#   NormalizeAncestorName hands back the type arguments. All of that decides
#   WHICH type_ancestors row is written, so it is DERIVED rows only, no parse
#   change: remedy is index --all --resolve-only.
#
#   ABSORBS AN UNPINNED BUMP. 1.2.0 -> 1.3.0 (bc2e39dc, 2026-09-16: member-access
#   refs bind properties/fields and accessor calls earn call_edges rows) moved
#   the constant but never re-pinned this baseline, so the active line below was
#   still 1.2.0 while the source declared 1.3.0. The guard reported "changed AND
#   bumped" on every run since -- green, but no longer pinning anything. This
#   entry pins 1.4.0 against the current surface and closes that gap.
#
#   Superseded: 1.2.0-alpha|6f0b03699c2cb23f83c8b5831a0013c92cacbea98da8759f033fd528acad2f2f

# 2026-09-17: hash re-pinned WITHIN 1.4.0-alpha (final-review wave, ruling R19:
#   no version constant moves in this wave). No index has yet been resolved
#   under 1.4.0, so the pin moves within the same version instead of bumping
#   -- there is no 1.4.0 edge anywhere for a bump to invalidate. What moved:
#   GenericArity now counts PARAMETERS, not constraint commas
#   (<T: class, constructor> = 1; <S: IUnknown; I: IUnknown> = 2), which is
#   the number FilterCandidatesByArity compares, so every edge onto a
#   constrained generic resolves differently (final review #1); the manifest
#   gained the three Core.Model helpers that decide which edge is written
#   (GenericArity, SplitGenericName, StripGenericSegments -- the last moved
#   here from the parser) and GetTransitiveAncestors (late resolution), and
#   EXCLUDEs PreferArity (query-side reader); src\index CallResolver.
#   FindChildOfKind declines on same-named locals of differing Signature.
#   The guard itself changed too: spans are cut from the implementation
#   section only (Core.Model declares free functions at column 1 in its
#   interface, which would otherwise hash the next routine's doc comment),
#   the drift vocabulary gained arity|generic, and the drift walk covers
#   Core.Model. Remedy when an index exists: index --all --resolve-only.
#
#   Superseded: 1.4.0-alpha|2cda913770535660645b65f9daceb3483b729158dbdf64d2836be374149f536c
#
# 2026-09-17: hash re-pinned WITHIN 1.4.0-alpha (batch residue; ruling R19
#   still holds -- no index has been resolved under 1.4.0 yet, the overnight
#   rebuild is the first). Commit "fix(resolve): ResolveAccessor narrows
#   overloaded accessors by arity or declines; FindSymbolsByExactName strips
#   generics per dotted segment (batch residue)". What moved: src\index
#   CallResolver.ResolveAccessor no longer first-matches an OVERLOADED
#   accessor set -- PickAccessor keeps the candidates whose arity range
#   contains the property's index count (+1 for a setter, read off the
#   declaring line by PropertyIndexArity) and answers only a unique survivor;
#   same-count overloads decline (accessor 0, no call edge) and a declined set
#   on the declaring class stops the ancestor walk. FindSymbolsByExactName is
#   a READER and is not in the manifest. Remedy when an index exists:
#   index --all --resolve-only.
#
#   Superseded: 1.4.0-alpha|a35513046797f2f0cf416752672496d55bec00368bb9e08238e0279eb4004389

# 2026-09-19: hash re-pinned WITHIN 1.4.0-alpha for the COMMENT-ONLY autodoc
#   regen 1307e778 (document --project src\cli\drag-lint.dproj --apply after
#   the v23 rebuild). That commit re-pinned the EXTRACTOR baseline with the
#   comment-only note but NOT this one, so this guard has been red on main
#   since 2026-09-17 (bisected: d318c2c1 PASS, 1307e778 FAIL). What moved the
#   hash: DRagLint.Index.CallResolver.pas is hashed WHOLE (`::*`), doc comments
#   included, and its <!-- drag-lint:auto --> blocks were regenerated. Verified
#   0 non-/// lines changed across src\index, Storage.SQLite and Core.Model
#   between d318c2c1 and 1307e778 -- nothing resolves differently, no index owes
#   a re-resolve. Same discipline as the 2026-09-07 autodoc entry above. Ruling
#   (coordinator, 2026-09-19): do not narrow the `::*` entry, do not move the
#   constant.
#
#   Superseded: 1.4.0-alpha|c1d56f481b2b1107635f79319f399f9c5b822474a0b9752b1bd5a0c838d304fa

# 2026-09-19: manifest WIDENED (FindSymbolsByExactName / FoldStubs / the
#   DRagLint.Core.ForwardStub unit added as resolve surface after the C2.5
#   review); code on the previously-listed surface unchanged. WHY THEY ARE
#   SURFACE: DRagLint.Index.CallResolver.pas:2176 calls FindSymbolsByExactName
#   on the extra stores for a receiver's type and declines on Found <> 1, so
#   what that reader returns decides which call_edges are written -- the
#   manifest's own closure rule. The hash moves only because the manifest grew
#   (three new hashed spans); every span that was already listed hashes to what
#   it did before. The C2.5 behaviour change itself (RTL forward stubs such as
#   TComponent/TReader/TWriter were 2 rows and are now 1, so cross-store
#   receivers that used to decline now resolve) is recorded in CHANGELOG
#   Unreleased and awaits the owner's bump decision; until then an index
#   resolved before C2.5 lacks those edges (remedy when the owner bumps:
#   index --all --resolve-only). Found by the review, not by the drift regex.
#
#   Superseded: 1.4.0-alpha|cc8c86896c828038e9eeb9102faa988fe9aede73f4c6aa0eef65b7049322c2e0

# 2026-09-21: 1.4.0-alpha -> 1.5.0-alpha (purity v2, plan C6.1). A new `purity`
#   resolve stage -- src\analysis\DRagLint.Analysis.PurityStage.pas over the
#   pure model in src\analysis\DRagLint.Analysis.Purity.pas -- derives
#   symbol_facts.effect_free / effect_summary / effect_witness from call_edges,
#   member_accesses, refs and routine bodies the index already holds. DERIVED
#   columns only, no parse change: remedy is index --all --resolve-only.
#
#   THE MANIFEST WAS WIDENED, AND A LITERAL READING OF THE HEADER ABOVE WOULD
#   HAVE REFUSED IT. That header scopes the resolve surface to "the code that
#   DERIVES call_edges, type_ancestors, type_helpers and unit_uses targets", and
#   the purity stage derives none of those -- it writes symbol_facts columns.
#   Both analysis units go in anyway, and the reason is the staleness mechanics
#   rather than the list of tables:
#
#     TPurityStage recomputes ONLY when PurityNeedsRun is True, i.e. only while
#     some row with body_loc > 0 still has effect_free IS NULL. Once a database
#     has been FILLED by a stage, every row has a verdict, PurityNeedsRun goes
#     False, and that database keeps the OLD verdicts forever. There is no
#     second trigger -- not a changed file, not a re-lint, not a re-resolve of
#     the call pass. The resolver fingerprint is the ONLY thing left that can
#     force it to think again.
#
#   So without these entries, any future change to purity logic goes silently
#   stale in every index: the columns are populated, the answers look complete,
#   and they are the previous build's. That is verbatim the failure mode this
#   file's own header calls out -- "the failure mode of getting it wrong is
#   SILENT" -- and it is why the entry is judged by what the stamp PROTECTS
#   rather than by which four tables the header happens to name.
#
#   THIS IS THE SAME PRINCIPLE AS THE 2026-09-19 WIDENING, ONE STEP FURTHER.
#   That entry admitted a READER the resolve pass depends on (FindSymbolsByExactName
#   / FoldStubs), on the manifest's own closure rule: a routine that decides
#   what is WRITTEN is surface even though it writes nothing. This entry extends
#   the same rule from readers the pass depends on to WRITES the pass performs
#   -- a second derived-data producer, sharing the one resolve stamp because it
#   shares the one remedy and the one silent-staleness hazard.
#
#   WHAT ELSE MOVED THE HASH, so the diff is not mistaken for code drift:
#   the manifest gained the two analysis units (hashed whole, `::*`) plus the
#   store surface the stage uses -- PutEffectFacts, PurityNeedsRun,
#   GetAllSymbolFacts, FindSymbolsWithFacts, DumpAllMemberAccesses -- and
#   DumpAllCallEdges MOVED OUT OF THE EXCLUSION LIST. It had been excluded as a
#   query-side reader on the stated ground that "a reader cannot make stored
#   data stale"; PurityStage.pas:578 now loads every call edge through it, one
#   line above the DumpAllMemberAccesses call, so that ground is false and the
#   exclusion's reason no longer held. Every span that was already listed hashes
#   to what it hashed before; no previously-listed code changed in this commit.
#
#   KNOWN AND DELIBERATE GAP, recorded in the manifest beside the entries: the
#   stage reads eight further store routines (FindAllChildSymbols,
#   FindChildSymbolByName, FindSymbolsByKind, GetAllFileStamps, GetFilePath,
#   GetReferencesFromFile, GetSymbolById, and GetTransitiveAncestors which was
#   already listed). Strict closure puts all of them in; widening that far is a
#   scoping decision larger than this stage and is left for the owner. It is
#   written down rather than skipped so that a purity verdict changing without
#   this guard noticing is diagnosable instead of mysterious.
#
#   THE C2.5 CROSS-STORE QUESTION RECORDED IN THE 2026-09-19 ENTRY IS NOT
#   SETTLED HERE. That bump still awaits the owner's decision; this bump is
#   about purity and carries no verdict on it. An index re-resolved under
#   1.5.0-alpha does, as a side effect, pick the C2.5 edges up -- worth knowing,
#   but not a decision this entry is making.
#
#   Superseded: 1.4.0-alpha|463989bb0a729a967964a9a29ec5913d23133ed5eb6d1d5611b3a89c1acee2d3

1.5.0-alpha|690833814ff9efb59db95b51343d52e17ab8b759650cc5a9605da2095f9b3cdf
